.\" ncsel(1) manual page
.Dd April 24, 2026
.Dt NCSEL 1
.Os
.Sh NAME
.Nm ncsel
.Nd ncurses-based terminal selector
.Sh SYNOPSIS
.Nm
.Op Fl bhmsn
.Op Fl f Ar file
.Op Ar file ...
.Sh DESCRIPTION
.Nm
is an ncurses-based pager and selector. It reads input from standard
input or from a file and displays the content in an
interactive terminal interface. The user can move through the list,
select entries, and return the selected line(s) or line number(s) to
the calling script.

The tool is intended for use in POSIX shell scripts where an
interactive selector is useful. Depending on the options,
.Nm
can return selected lines, their line numbers, or both. When
multiselect mode is enabled, multiple entries may be marked and
returned.

.Nm
requires either ncurses or ncursesw.
.Sh OPTIONS
.Bl -tag -width Ds
.It Fl b
Display the selection using reverse video instead of a cursor-style
highlight.
.It Fl h
Show usage information.
.It Fl m
Enable multiselect mode. When enabled, entries may be marked or
unmarked.
.It Fl n
Return only the line number of the selected entry.
.It Fl s
Return only the selected line itself. By default, both the line number
and the line content are returned.
.It Fl f Ar file
Read input from
.Ar file .
Use
.Sq -
to read from standard input. 
.El
.Sh INTERACTIVE KEYS
While viewing a list, the following keys are available:
.Bl -tag -width Ds
.It f, ^F, SPACE, PageDown
Move forward one window.
.It b, ^B, ESC-v, PageUp
Move backward one window.
.It j, ^N, Down
Move down one line.
.It k, ^P, Up
Move up one line.
.It Enter
Select and exit.
.It q
Exit without selection (return value 0).
.It x
Exit without selection (return value -1).
.It m, +
Toggle mark (multiselect mode only).
.It !
Execute a command. The variable %S contains the line
the cursor is on. Example: printf "%S" | xclip
.El

Exiting with
.Sq x
returns -1 and can be used as a “go back” signal in scripts.
.Sh EXAMPLES
Select a file from a directory listing:
.Bd -literal -offset indent
ls -a . | ncsel -
.Ed

Select from a list of strings:
.Bd -literal -offset indent
printf "%s\\n%s\\n%s\\n" "One" "Two" "Three" | ncsel -bf -
.Ed

Possible usage in a sh script:
.Bd -literal -offset indent
key=$(printf "${radiostations}" | ncsel -b -)
.Ed
.Sh BUGS
Probably.
